Received: "EngineSettingsSimulationFlying: Coupled engine plant · JSBSim 1.2.4-fork.21 ?JSBSim's coupled engine plant: fuel, ignition, combustion, gas path, shafts, nozzle, metal temperatures and the lift fan advance together. Reheat thrust exists only while reheat fuel burns.?Engine modelCoupled engine plantEmpirical tablesHow the engine is simulated, on an aircraft that offers both. The coupled engine plant advances fuel, combustion, gas path, shafts, nozzle and lift fan together. Empirical tables is the aircraft's earlier JSBSim table engine, unchanged, for flying one after the other.Now Coupled engine plant (default). Default Coupled engine plant: The plant gives reheat thrust only while reheat fuel burns, and one fuel and energy account for the engine and lift fan. Applies on the next start.osfs.engine.model?Gas path algorithmReducedComponentHow the coupled engine solves its gas path each step. Reduced uses tabulated gas properties and reuses its Jacobian, and falls back to the component reference on a step it cannot close. Component evaluates the reference on every step, at several times the work.Now Reduced (default). Default Reduced: It tracks the component reference within the validated tolerance at a fraction of its work.osfs.enginePlant.algorithmiterations/step?Iteration capNewton iterations one engine step may spend, every solve and the fallback included; a quarter is kept for publishing the step. A step that runs out is not accepted, and the engine retries it with a held-back nozzle and smaller substeps.Now 24 iterations/step (default). Default 24 iterations/step: The first step of an engine at rest in moving air needs about 12 component iterations after the reduced attempt; 24 covers it with margin, and an accepted step usually takes 2 to 6.osfs.enginePlant.iterationCapsubsteps?Substep capHow many substeps a step that fails may be divided into before it is reported as failed.Now 4 substeps (default). Default 4 substeps: Steps at 120 Hz have closed without substeps in every recorded transient; four allows halving twice.osfs.enginePlant.subdivisionCap?Solve toleranceScaled residual every gas-path solve must reach. Smaller is more accurate and costs more iterations.Now 0 (default). Default 0: At 1e-6 the per-step energy ledger closes within 1e-5 and the reduced algorithm matches the reference.osfs.enginePlant.toleranceKiB?Reduced-model memoryMemory the reduced algorithm may use for its gas tables, per engine. Below what its tables need, the engine uses the component reference.Now 1,024 KiB (default). Default 1,024 KiB: The tables take 21 KiB; 1 MiB leaves room without mattering beside the rest of the app.osfs.enginePlant.closureBudgetKiBShow all parametersSave as presetSaveCancel?Fuel flow unitlb/hgal/hThe unit the HUD engine line and the Engine tab show fuel flow in; clicking the HUD's fuel flow switches it.Now lb/h (default). Default lb/h: The original unit.osfs.engineMonitor.fuelFlowUnitframes/s?Shaft animation FPS ceilingShaft dots follow this screen's animation frames up to this ceiling, independently of globe drawing and numeric readouts. Zero disables their renderer. Hidden, paused and unchanged indicators do not draw.Now 1,000 frames/s (default). Default 1,000 frames/s: A high ceiling lets the browser use the screen's refresh cadence, including 60, 120, 144 and 240 Hz. It does not request 1000 frames/s from the browser; lower it to spend less GPU work.osfs.engineMonitor.orbFpsrev/s?Maximum displayed shaft speedRequested upper limit in visual revolutions per simulated second at maximum modeled speed. Both shafts share a scale limited by this client's actual drawing cadence and the hotspot pattern step limit; the Engine tab shows the effective speed and reason. This is a scaled speed cue, not real turbine RPM.Now 2 rev/s (default). Default 2 rev/s: A two-revolution ceiling makes high engine speed visibly fast; the pattern step limit protects the two rotating hotspots when the client draws too slowly to show that speed.osfs.engineMonitor.orbTurnsPerSecondpattern pitches/frame?Maximum hotspot pattern step per frameMaximum advance of the two-hotspot pattern per actual drawn frame, in pattern pitches. One pitch is half a revolution. Both shafts share the resulting speed scale. Staying below half a pitch preserves the hotspot cue's forward direction between submitted frames; individual blades may still alias. The Engine tab shows the effective limit.Now 0.45 pattern pitches/frame (default). Default 0.45 pattern pitches/frame: A 0.45-pitch limit uses most of the available hotspot motion range while leaving a margin below the ambiguous half-pitch boundary.osfs.engineMonitor.orbMaxPatternSteppx/CSSpx?Shaft indicator resolutionMaximum drawing pixels per CSS pixel for the shaft dots, capped by the display's pixel density. More pixels increase GPU work and memory.Now 2 px/CSSpx (default). Default 2 px/CSSpx: A two-times density cap keeps the small dots clear with a small drawing surface.osfs.engineMonitor.orbPixelRatioShow all parametersSave as presetSaveCancels?Engine history windowSimulation time retained for the Engine tab's plots and CSV export. The buffer is bounded by this window and the sampling rate; resetting simulation time starts a new history.Now 60 s (default). Default 60 s: One minute shows spool transients while bounding diagnostic memory.osfs.engineMonitor.historySecondsHz?Engine history sampling ceilingMaximum samples per simulated second, limited by available model updates. Zero disables capture and retains bounded history. Current readings keep updating. Hidden plots do not draw; pause adds no samples.Now 5 Hz (default). Default 5 Hz: Five samples per second resolve slow spool and thermal trends with a small buffer. These plots do not measure combustion or acoustic oscillations.osfs.engineMonitor.historyHzmetrics?History metric budgetMaximum retained engine variables. New recordings enter in registry order; admitted recordings keep their place. Lowering a budget removes buffers from the end of admission order. The live capture status counts waiting variables.Now 32 metrics (default). Default 32 metrics: Thirty-two variables cover the ordinary engine traces and several diagnostics without retaining every published property.osfs.engineMonitor.historyMetricsKiB?History storage budgetUpper bound on retained numeric history buffers. Each metric reserves the selected window and sampling ceiling. Lowering the budget removes whole metric buffers from the end of admission order; waiting recordings and allocated bytes are shown in Live data.Now 4,096 KiB (default). Default 4,096 KiB: Four MiB provides room for ordinary histories while keeping a firm bound when the window, sample ceiling or recorded catalog grows.osfs.engineMonitor.historyMemoryKiB?Continue hidden history captureContinue recording admitted variables when their plot, family or Engine tab is closed. Turning this off pauses hidden capture except explicitly requested solid heat balances; current readings still update and retained samples remain available.Now on (default). Default on: Closing a view does not silently create history gaps; disable hidden capture explicitly to reduce sampling work.osfs.engineMonitor.hiddenHistory?Record solid heat balancesRequest available native per-solid heat rates, bath temperatures and timestep energy receipts for capture within the recording budgets. Display samples cannot reconstruct every fixed-step energy transfer.Now off (default). Default off: Keep ordinary history compact; enable additional recordings when diagnosing native heating. Their live readings remain available independently of capture.osfs.engineMonitor.thermalHistoryShow all parametersSave as presetSaveCancel1 to 100 m?Test stand zoom limitsThe nearest and farthest inspection camera distances from the isolated engine assembly.Now 1 to 100 m (default). Default 1 to 100 m: The isolated nozzle can be inspected much closer than the full aircraft.osfs.engineTest.zoomLimitsm?Test stand starting view distanceInitial camera distance from the isolated engine geometry when opening the test stand. Normal orbit and zoom controls remain available.Now 6 m (default). Default 6 m: Six metres frames the nozzle and its exhaust for inspection. Applies on the next start.osfs.engineTest.viewDistanceMetersShow all parametersSave as presetSaveCancelLive data"

